WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.214
Welcome to Module 1 of the
Attribute-Driven Migration learning path:

2
00:00:04.214 --> 00:00:07.575
send HR records through a
middleware that fails closed.

3
00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:12.267
The path teaches the workbook's book
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration,

4
00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:16.525
one chapter per module, in the order
of the implementation guide's gates.

5
00:00:16.525 --> 00:00:18.000
There are nine modules.

6
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.850
This one serves Gates 2 and 3, and takes
Chapter 01, the HR-to-Entra middleware.

7
00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:29.740
You'll see what the middleware owns
and what the provisioning service owns,

8
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.815
what Microsoft's CSV2SCIM
sample leaves to you,

9
00:00:32.815 --> 00:00:37.153
and how the hardened client refuses
bad rows, orders leavers first,

10
00:00:37.153 --> 00:00:41.175
holds a mass disable and ties
every batch to its inputs by hash.

11
00:00:41.175 --> 00:00:45.150
Then you'll run it on your laptop
against a fictional HR extract.

12
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:50.890
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not against a production tenant.

13
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.678
Why start here?

14
00:00:55.678 --> 00:00:59.565
Every later gate trusts
the attributes HR sends.

15
00:00:59.565 --> 00:01:03.953
A bad HR record becomes bad access
faster than any ticket queue.

16
00:01:03.953 --> 00:01:09.641
So the guide proves the values true at Gate
2, and maps them through sync at Gate 3.

17
00:01:09.641 --> 00:01:13.232
The middleware, between the
HR extract and Entra ID,

18
00:01:13.232 --> 00:01:15.627
is where a record is first checked.

19
00:01:15.627 --> 00:01:20.353
Microsoft's CSV2SCIM sample
sends every row it reads.

20
00:01:20.353 --> 00:01:24.794
That's by design: it's a converter, and
Microsoft Learn's page on API-driven

21
00:01:24.794 --> 00:01:30.065
inbound provisioning with PowerShell lists
validation as a customization you add.

22
00:01:30.065 --> 00:01:33.428
In this module you see what a
federal deployment has to add,

23
00:01:33.428 --> 00:01:35.740
and you run the client that adds it.

24
00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.330
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

25
00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.977
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

26
00:01:44.977 --> 00:01:49.406
You'll explain what the middleware owns
and what the provisioning service owns,

27
00:01:49.406 --> 00:01:51.352
and what a two-oh-two Accepted means.

28
00:01:51.352 --> 00:01:55.802
You'll describe what Microsoft's
CSV2SCIM sample leaves to you.

29
00:01:55.802 --> 00:01:58.915
You'll run the hardened
client and read its counts,

30
00:01:58.915 --> 00:02:01.040
its quarantine and its exit codes.

31
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.640
You'll verify a run's provenance by hash,

32
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.565
and show that a rerun
gives identical bytes.

33
00:02:06.565 --> 00:02:11.627
And you'll explain why the client sends
every record whole and holds a mass disable.

34
00:02:11.627 --> 00:02:15.713
Before you start, work through
Microsoft Automation Module 1,

35
00:02:15.713 --> 00:02:18.516
for the clouds, sign-in
and least privilege.

36
00:02:18.516 --> 00:02:23.590
Lab 1 needs Python 3.10 or later
with pytest, and no tenant.

37
00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:27.709
PowerShell 7 with Pester 5 is
optional: it runs the PowerShell

38
00:02:27.709 --> 00:02:32.520
client and Microsoft's sample,
which the full chapter lab compares.

39
00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.520
Start with the split.

40
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.195
The HR system writes a CSV extract.

41
00:02:41.195 --> 00:02:44.369
The middleware reads it,
and sends SCIM bulk requests

42
00:02:44.369 --> 00:02:47.182
to the provisioning job's
bulk upload endpoint.

43
00:02:47.182 --> 00:02:51.888
The provisioning service owns the
mapping from SCIM attributes to directory

44
00:02:51.888 --> 00:02:56.521
attributes, the matching of incoming
records to existing users, and the write.

45
00:02:56.521 --> 00:03:00.511
The middleware owns everything
before the POST: what is sent,

46
00:03:00.511 --> 00:03:03.783
in what order, how fast, and
what record is kept of it.

47
00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:06.673
Microsoft Learn's page
on the PowerShell sample

48
00:03:06.673 --> 00:03:09.420
gives that split as
Microsoft's design intent.

49
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.138
And one thing to remember from this slide:

50
00:03:12.138 --> 00:03:16.695
Learn's bulk upload API reference
says the endpoint is asynchronous.

51
00:03:16.695 --> 00:03:19.776
A two-oh-two Accepted means
the batch was staged for

52
00:03:19.776 --> 00:03:22.857
the provisioning job, not
that any user was written.

53
00:03:22.857 --> 00:03:25.842
The Location header points
at the provisioning logs,

54
00:03:25.842 --> 00:03:28.170
where each record's result appears.

55
00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.466
Microsoft publishes a PowerShell
sample for this integration,

56
00:03:36.466 --> 00:03:39.982
CSV2SCIM, with a full
tutorial on Microsoft Learn.

57
00:03:39.982 --> 00:03:43.559
Use it; the repository
keeps a pinned copy of

58
00:03:43.559 --> 00:03:47.433
Microsoft's sample at
upstream commit f2e8c59.

59
00:03:47.433 --> 00:03:52.557
Run on the book's HR fixture,
nineteen rows, it sends all nineteen.

60
00:03:52.557 --> 00:03:54.008
Here's what it leaves to you.

61
00:03:54.008 --> 00:03:56.577
It validates the mapping, not the values;

62
00:03:56.577 --> 00:04:01.482
the hardened client fails closed, and
quarantines nine of the nineteen rows.

63
00:04:01.482 --> 00:04:04.991
It sends in file order; the
client sends leavers first,

64
00:04:04.991 --> 00:04:07.383
then managers before their reports.

65
00:04:07.383 --> 00:04:09.521
It has no limit on mass change.

66
00:04:09.521 --> 00:04:12.934
Its endpoint is hard-coded
to the worldwide beta host,

67
00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:17.435
and Learn's page on Microsoft Graph
national cloud deployments gives

68
00:04:17.435 --> 00:04:21.470
other hosts for GCC High and DoD,
so it can't reach them as written.

69
00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:25.023
Its sign-in asks for Application
dot ReadWrite dot All,

70
00:04:25.023 --> 00:04:27.470
which can change any app in the tenant.

71
00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:33.560
And each bulk I D is a new random GUID,
so a batch can't be traced to its inputs.

72
00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:40.010
The client takes each
row and either builds

73
00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:42.710
a SCIM operation or
refuses it with reasons.

74
00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:45.820
A row is refused when a
required column is empty,

75
00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:51.004
the person number doesn't fit employee I
D, the worker type is unknown or refused,

76
00:04:51.004 --> 00:04:54.353
such as a pending worker, a
code isn't in the code list,

77
00:04:54.353 --> 00:04:59.457
a value is longer than the target attribute
holds, a date doesn't parse, a worker

78
00:04:59.457 --> 00:05:04.960
is recorded as their own manager, or an
inactive assignment has no termination date.

79
00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.040
Every refused row goes
to the quarantine file,

80
00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.335
with its reasons, the unchanged
HR row and the attempted payload.

81
00:05:12.335 --> 00:05:14.172
These rules are the kit's design.

82
00:05:14.172 --> 00:05:15.735
Some are warnings instead.

83
00:05:15.735 --> 00:05:20.785
A suspended assignment keeps the account
active, because leave is not a leaver.

84
00:05:20.785 --> 00:05:24.548
A manager separating in the same
run never blocks the joiner.

85
00:05:24.548 --> 00:05:28.108
And this workbook's guidance
is to treat every new rule

86
00:05:28.108 --> 00:05:32.110
as a refusal until the HR owner
agrees it can be a warning.

87
00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:37.072
Three more rules.

88
00:05:37.072 --> 00:05:40.122
First, send the complete
record every time.

89
00:05:40.122 --> 00:05:44.673
Microsoft Learn's page on API-driven
inbound provisioning concepts

90
00:05:44.673 --> 00:05:48.585
says to include the whole user
record in every bulk request.

91
00:05:48.585 --> 00:05:52.833
When the job's clear attribute
values feature, a preview, is on,

92
00:05:52.833 --> 00:05:57.760
a null or empty value clears the
target, and so can an omitted attribute.

93
00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.097
So the client never
sends a partial record.

94
00:06:00.097 --> 00:06:01.523
Second, order.

95
00:06:01.523 --> 00:06:06.347
Leavers go first, then managers before
their reports, then person key.

96
00:06:06.347 --> 00:06:10.270
When one person has several
rows, a single separation wins

97
00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:14.273
and the rest are superseded;
any other duplicate is refused.

98
00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:19.460
The bulk upload API reference allows
at most fifty operations per request.

99
00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:20.910
Third, the hold.

100
00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:25.845
Two guards hold the whole run, with exit
code three, until someone confirms it:

101
00:06:25.845 --> 00:06:30.310
more disables than the run allows,
or more calls than the daily budget.

102
00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:34.049
The budget follows the quota
on Learn's concepts page:

103
00:06:34.049 --> 00:06:39.520
two thousand calls a day with P1 or P2,
and six thousand with ID Governance.

104
00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:48.045
The last idea is the record, and it's
the kit's design, not Microsoft's.

105
00:06:48.045 --> 00:06:52.633
The provenance file ties every batch
to the exact inputs that produced it.

106
00:06:52.633 --> 00:06:57.315
It holds the hash of the extract and the
hash of the mapping file, the counts,

107
00:06:57.315 --> 00:07:00.309
whether the run was held,
each batch with its own

108
00:07:00.309 --> 00:07:03.457
hash and its person keys,
and the quarantined keys.

109
00:07:03.457 --> 00:07:08.320
Each batch hash is the same one the apply
results record when that batch is sent.

110
00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:12.935
So an auditor can go from a line in the
provisioning logs to the person key,

111
00:07:12.935 --> 00:07:17.320
to the batch and its Location, to the
extract and mapping files by hash.

112
00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:22.020
That only works because each
operation's bulk I D is the person key.

113
00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.554
With a random bulk I D, as in the sample,

114
00:07:24.554 --> 00:07:27.932
the same input would never
produce the same hash.

115
00:07:27.932 --> 00:07:33.220
The manifest carries keys and hashes
only, so it can sit in the evidence store.

116
00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:37.695
Try it.

117
00:07:37.695 --> 00:07:41.331
Lab 1 runs in the hcm-entra
folder of the examples,

118
00:07:41.331 --> 00:07:45.695
on a fictional HR extract, and
nothing in it sends a request.

119
00:07:45.695 --> 00:07:50.383
Step one runs the hardened client with
an as-of date of October twenty-fifth.

120
00:07:50.383 --> 00:07:54.754
It prints its counts: nine
rows ready, nine quarantined,

121
00:07:54.754 --> 00:07:59.708
one superseded, one batch, three
disables, and the run isn't held.

122
00:07:59.708 --> 00:08:03.121
The command exits two,
because rows were quarantined.

123
00:08:03.121 --> 00:08:07.157
Step two prints the quarantine,
one line per refused row.

124
00:08:07.157 --> 00:08:08.358
A pending worker.

125
00:08:08.358 --> 00:08:09.933
An unknown worker type.

126
00:08:09.933 --> 00:08:11.983
A row with no person number at all.

127
00:08:11.983 --> 00:08:15.782
A seventeen-character key that
doesn't fit employee I D.

128
00:08:15.782 --> 00:08:18.057
A worker recorded as their own manager.

129
00:08:18.057 --> 00:08:20.908
An inactive assignment
with no termination date.

130
00:08:20.908 --> 00:08:25.681
A department name that's too long, a position
category that isn't in the code list,

131
00:08:25.681 --> 00:08:28.032
and a department code with a space in it.

132
00:08:28.032 --> 00:08:32.230
Every one of those,
CSV2SCIM would have sent.

133
00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:38.155
Step three checks the provenance.

134
00:08:38.155 --> 00:08:41.443
Show the manifest, then
hash the extract yourself.

135
00:08:41.443 --> 00:08:45.512
The hash you compute, starting
c a oh six b one one one,

136
00:08:45.512 --> 00:08:49.193
is the same as the extract
hash in the manifest.

137
00:08:49.193 --> 00:08:51.679
So the record really is tied to this file.

138
00:08:51.679 --> 00:08:53.766
Look at the batch's person keys, too:

139
00:08:53.766 --> 00:08:57.342
they start with the leaver,
one hundred thousand and three.

140
00:08:57.342 --> 00:08:58.518
Leavers first.

141
00:08:58.518 --> 00:09:02.342
Step four runs the client again,
into a second output folder,

142
00:09:02.342 --> 00:09:06.167
and compares the bytes of the
bulk request and the manifest.

143
00:09:06.167 --> 00:09:07.268
They're identical.

144
00:09:07.268 --> 00:09:09.964
That's the property the bulk I D buys you:

145
00:09:09.964 --> 00:09:13.504
the same input gives the
same batch hash, every time.

146
00:09:13.504 --> 00:09:19.255
Microsoft's sample gives a new bulk I D
on every run, so its hashes never repeat.

147
00:09:19.255 --> 00:09:23.820
The long commands on the slide are
wrapped, and the manifest is trimmed.

148
00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:29.858
Step five breaks a row on purpose.

149
00:09:29.858 --> 00:09:33.260
The sed command writes a copy
of the extract with worker one

150
00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:36.594
hundred thousand and five's
department code in lower case.

151
00:09:36.594 --> 00:09:41.343
Run the client on it, and the counts
become eight ready and ten quarantined,

152
00:09:41.343 --> 00:09:45.157
and the quarantine says why: the
lower-case value isn't a code.

153
00:09:45.157 --> 00:09:47.691
One small typo in HR,
and the record stops at

154
00:09:47.691 --> 00:09:50.732
the middleware instead of
reaching the directory.

155
00:09:50.732 --> 00:09:52.332
Step six holds a run.

156
00:09:52.332 --> 00:09:57.032
A settings file allows only one disable
per run, and the fixture has three.

157
00:09:57.032 --> 00:10:00.629
So the client holds the whole
run, with exit code three,

158
00:10:00.629 --> 00:10:05.207
and says why: three disables exceed
the limit, held for confirmation.

159
00:10:05.207 --> 00:10:09.583
Nothing is sent until someone confirms
it with the confirm large delta option.

160
00:10:09.583 --> 00:10:12.682
Step seven runs the tests: seventy pass.

161
00:10:12.682 --> 00:10:14.430
Step eight cleans up.

162
00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:23.216
Lab 2 runs the client against a
test tenant, never production,

163
00:10:23.216 --> 00:10:27.043
with Microsoft Entra ID
P1, P2 or ID Governance.

164
00:10:27.043 --> 00:10:33.842
Create an API-driven inbound provisioning
app, and extend its schema with CSV2SCIM.

165
00:10:33.842 --> 00:10:35.872
Grant the sender one permission,

166
00:10:35.872 --> 00:10:39.330
with a certificate or a managed
identity, never a secret.

167
00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:42.491
Send five fictional records
with the hardened client,

168
00:10:42.491 --> 00:10:45.442
and reconcile them against
the provisioning logs.

169
00:10:45.442 --> 00:10:48.179
Remember the two-oh-two:
the reconciliation is

170
00:10:48.179 --> 00:10:51.205
where you find out whether
each record was written.

171
00:10:51.205 --> 00:10:55.582
To back it out, step seven sends
the five records again as inactive,

172
00:10:55.582 --> 00:10:59.230
with a termination date, and
unsets the token variable.

173
00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:01.380
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

174
00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:05.210
Follow Chapter 01's Lab 2, step by step.

175
00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:10.347
The federal requirement.

176
00:11:10.347 --> 00:11:13.671
The middleware is where an HR
record becomes an account,

177
00:11:13.671 --> 00:11:16.561
so its outputs are
account-management evidence.

178
00:11:16.561 --> 00:11:20.359
The validation report
supports input validation,

179
00:11:20.359 --> 00:11:23.073
S I ten, and account management, A C two.

180
00:11:23.073 --> 00:11:25.925
The quarantine supports
identifier management,

181
00:11:25.925 --> 00:11:30.447
and lives in the access-controlled audit
store, because it holds personal data.

182
00:11:30.447 --> 00:11:33.476
The provenance manifest
supports configuration

183
00:11:33.476 --> 00:11:36.360
change control and the
content of audit records.

184
00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.285
A confirmed mass disable
leaves a change record.

185
00:11:39.285 --> 00:11:42.297
The tests and the pinned
sample's snapshot support

186
00:11:42.297 --> 00:11:45.022
developer testing and
supply chain controls.

187
00:11:45.022 --> 00:11:49.015
And Lab 2 adds the sender's app
registration and consent record,

188
00:11:49.015 --> 00:11:52.547
and the reconciliation
against the provisioning logs.

189
00:11:52.547 --> 00:11:58.398
The controls are from NIST SP
800-53, in its Moderate baseline.

190
00:11:58.398 --> 00:12:03.610
Key Security Indicators are FedRAMP 20x
indicators for cloud service providers;

191
00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:07.447
the agency lines its evidence up
with what its providers report.

192
00:12:07.447 --> 00:12:11.370
Which output supports which
control is this workbook's reading.

193
00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:17.095
Check yourself with two questions.

194
00:12:17.095 --> 00:12:21.332
First: the bulk upload endpoint
answers two-oh-two Accepted.

195
00:12:21.332 --> 00:12:22.521
What does that tell you?

196
00:12:22.521 --> 00:12:26.276
The answer is b: the batch was
staged for the provisioning job,

197
00:12:26.276 --> 00:12:29.882
and each record's result appears
in the provisioning logs.

198
00:12:29.882 --> 00:12:34.740
Learn's bulk upload API reference
says the endpoint is asynchronous,

199
00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:38.320
so a two-oh-two is never
proof that a user was written.

200
00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:43.520
Second: a run would disable more accounts
than the maximum disables per run allows.

201
00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:44.532
What happens?

202
00:12:44.532 --> 00:12:48.240
The answer is c: the whole run
is held, with exit code three,

203
00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:52.432
until someone confirms it with
the confirm large delta option.

204
00:12:52.432 --> 00:12:54.595
You saw that in step six of the lab.

205
00:12:54.595 --> 00:12:59.492
The module page has two more questions,
on the bulk I D and on partial records,

206
00:12:59.492 --> 00:13:02.630
each linked to the
section that answers it.

207
00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:07.205
To sum up.

208
00:13:07.205 --> 00:13:12.200
You learned where the middleware's job ends
and the provisioning service's begins,

209
00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:16.167
and you ran the client that refuses
bad rows, orders leavers first,

210
00:13:16.167 --> 00:13:20.280
holds a mass disable, and keeps a
record an auditor can follow by hash.

211
00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:25.749
The evidence an ISSO keeps: the validation
report, the provenance manifest and,

212
00:13:25.749 --> 00:13:29.031
in Lab 2, the apply results,
in the evidence store;

213
00:13:29.031 --> 00:13:34.247
the quarantine in the access-controlled
audit store; the change record for

214
00:13:34.247 --> 00:13:39.380
any confirmed hold; the sender's app
registration; and the reconciliation.

215
00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:43.130
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 01 checklist.

216
00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:46.655
Next is Module 2: write
a validation kit check.

217
00:13:46.655 --> 00:13:48.801
You'll build and read V K SYNC oh two,

218
00:13:48.801 --> 00:13:52.711
the check that soft match and
hard-match takeover are blocked,

219
00:13:52.711 --> 00:13:57.140
and see why a read-only check still
asks for the least privilege it can.
